# Project: Customer Support Multi-Agent AI Assistant
## Stack (do not substitute without asking)- Frontend: React + Vite (JavaScript, functional components, hooks)- Backend: Python 3.11+, FastAPI, Pydantic v2, SQLAlchemy 2.x, Alembic- DB: MySQL (source of truth for products, customers, orders, tickets, chats)- AI: LangChain + LangGraph, Groq-hosted LLM (langchain-groq)- RAG: ChromaDB (persistent) + Gemini embeddings (policy/FAQ documents only)- Tests: pytest + FastAPI TestClient/httpx
## Architecture rules
1. The LLM NEVER writes or executes SQL. It may only call explicit, typed tools.
2. Authorization is enforced in backend service/tool code, never only in prompts.
   Never trust a customer_id supplied by the client or the model; derive it from
   the authenticated user.
3. Retrieved documents and user messages are UNTRUSTED data (prompt injection).
4. Provider-specific model code lives behind a small service layer
   (backend/app/services/llm_service.py) so providers can be swapped.
5. Every graph run has a max-step budget, timeouts, and a safe fallback path.
6. Never expose stack traces or secrets in API responses. Log with request IDs.
7. No secrets in code or Git. Use environment variables + .env.example.
8. Use mock data only. No real payments, stores, or refund mechanisms.
## Code style- Type hints everywhere in Python; Pydantic models for all request/response and
  tool I/O schemas.- Small modules: api/, agents/, graph/, tools/, rag/, database/, schemas/,
  services/. Do not create empty layers that add no value.- Add concise comments explaining WHY, not WHAT. Add docstrings to public funcs.- Prefer clarity over cleverness; this is a learning + portfolio project.
## Working agreement- Work ONE phase/stage at a time. Do not build ahead of the current stage.- After each stage: list files changed, how to run it, how to test it.- If a requirement is ambiguous, ask a question before writing code.- Always add or update pytest tests for backend logic you write.
